section: teardown
title: Teardown and leftovers
about: |
  What a run before can leave in the cloud and in Palette, and what a force delete leaves behind.
  The lab checks these with its inventory scripts before a build; a company checks its own estate.
rules:
- id: force-delete-leaves-cloud
  title: A force delete removes the records of Palette alone
  engines:
  - all
  tier: cloud
  severity: warn
  status: seen
  what: |
    A force delete removes the records of a cluster in Palette. The cloud keeps each instance,
    volume, and load balancer, and bills for them. Empty the cloud first. The reproduce lines
    read AWS and GCP by the cluster name; for another cloud, a company reads its own estate.
  reproduce:
    aws:
    - 'aws ec2 describe-instances --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --filters Name=tag-key,Values=sigs.k8s.io/cluster-api-provider-aws/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }} Name=instance-state-name,Values=pending,running,stopping,stopped,shutting-down --query ''Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId'' --output text   # no id before a force delete'
    - 'aws ec2 describe-volumes --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --filters Name=tag-key,Values=sigs.k8s.io/cluster-api-provider-aws/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }} --query ''Volumes[].VolumeId'' --output text   # no id before a force delete'
    - 'aws elb describe-load-balancers --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --query "LoadBalancerDescriptions[?LoadBalancerName==''{{ .entity.metadata.name }}-apiserver''].LoadBalancerName" --output text   # no name before a force delete'
    - 'aws ec2 describe-vpcs --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --filters Name=tag-key,Values=sigs.k8s.io/cluster-api-provider-aws/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }} --query ''Vpcs[].VpcId'' --output text   # the network goes last, minutes after the instances'
    eks:
    - 'aws ec2 describe-instances --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --filters Name=tag-key,Values=sigs.k8s.io/cluster-api-provider-aws/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }} Name=instance-state-name,Values=pending,running,stopping,stopped,shutting-down --query ''Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId'' --output text   # no id before a force delete'
    - 'aws ec2 describe-volumes --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --filters Name=tag-key,Values=sigs.k8s.io/cluster-api-provider-aws/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }} --query ''Volumes[].VolumeId'' --output text   # no id before a force delete'
    - 'aws ec2 describe-vpcs --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --filters Name=tag-key,Values=sigs.k8s.io/cluster-api-provider-aws/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }} --query ''Vpcs[].VpcId'' --output text   # the network goes last, minutes after the instances'
    - 'aws eks describe-cluster --name {{ .entity.metadata.name }} --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --query cluster.status   # ResourceNotFoundException means no cluster of the name'
    gcp:
    - 'gcloud compute instances list --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --filter ''name ~ ^{{ .entity.metadata.name }}'' --format ''value(name)''   # no line before a force delete'
    - 'gcloud compute disks list --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --filter ''name ~ ^{{ .entity.metadata.name }}'' --format ''value(name)''   # no line before a force delete'
    - 'gcloud compute forwarding-rules list --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --filter ''name ~ ^{{ .entity.metadata.name }}'' --format ''value(name)''   # the load balancer: no line before a force delete'
    - 'gcloud compute networks list --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --filter ''name ~ ^{{ .entity.metadata.name }}'' --format ''value(name)''   # the network goes last, minutes after the instances'
    gke:
    - 'gcloud container clusters list --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --filter ''name={{ .entity.metadata.name }}'' --format ''value(name,status)''   # no line means no cluster of the name'
    - 'gcloud compute instances list --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --filter ''name ~ ^gke-{{ .entity.metadata.name }}-'' --format ''value(name)''   # the nodes: no line before a force delete'
    - 'gcloud compute disks list --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --filter ''name ~ ^gke-{{ .entity.metadata.name }}-'' --format ''value(name)''   # no line before a force delete'
    all:
    - '# strata-preflight has no line for this cloud: count what the cloud holds under the cluster name {{ .entity.metadata.name }}, and find nothing before a force delete'
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Warn, or refuse a force delete while the cloud holds resources of the cluster.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: teardown lab
- id: teardown-order
  title: The network of a run before is gone
  engines:
  - aws
  - gcp
  tier: cloud
  severity: warn
  status: seen
  when: .clusterUid == null and .cloud == "aws"
  what: |
    A delete removes the instances first. The security groups, the route tables, the subnets, the
    gateway, and the network go last, minutes after. An inventory that stops at the instances
    says 0 too early. The check asks EC2 for a VPC with the Cluster API tag of the name; one
    found means the teardown before is not finished. For GCP, run the reproduce line by hand.
  check:
    kind: exec
    needs:
    - aws
    argv:
    - aws
    - ec2
    - describe-vpcs
    - --filters
    - Name=tag-key,Values=sigs.k8s.io/cluster-api-provider-aws/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }}
    - --region
    - '{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }}'
    - --output
    - json
    expr: |
      (.entity.metadata.name // "?") as $n | (.entity.spec.cloudConfig.region // "?") as $r
      | if .exec.exit != 0 then ["aws ec2 describe-vpcs failed: \(.exec.stderr | tostring | .[:200])"]
        else [ (.exec.stdout.Vpcs // [])[]
               | "VPC \(.VpcId) of a cluster named \($n) is still in \($r); the network goes last, so the teardown before is not finished. Wait until the inventory is 0 on every line" ]
        end
  reproduce:
    gcp:
    - 'gcloud compute networks list --project {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.project }} --filter ''name ~ ^{{ .entity.metadata.name }}'' --format json   # the network goes last'
  fix:
    owner: none
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    ref: docs/src/development/live-example.md
    note: on AWS, a deletion removes the security groups, route tables, subnets, internet gateway, and VPC last, minutes after the instances and the NAT gateway
  - kind: source
    note: the GCP inventory of the live example; a deletion removes the network last, minutes after the instances
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/aws/vpc-left.json
    clean: fixtures/aws/teardown-order-clean.json
- id: inventory-managed
  title: The inventory counts managed clusters
  engines:
  - eks
  - gke
  - lab
  tier: tool
  severity: warn
  status: seen
  what: |
    The lab inventory counted instances, disks, and networks, and not the EKS or GKE cluster, its
    nodes, or its node groups. It said 0 while a managed cluster still billed. #90 fixed it.
    `aws-inventory.sh` counts the EKS cluster of the name. `gcp-inventory.sh --gke` counts the
    GKE cluster and the `gke-<name>-` nodes, disks, and instance groups. A row of the lab.
  reproduce:
  - '# only the lab can reproduce this: the fault was in the inventory scripts of the live example'
  fix:
    owner: lab
    text: Count the managed cluster and its node groups in each inventory.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: '#90'
  - kind: source
    note: the GCP inventory of the live example; `--gke` counts the GKE cluster, which needs the Kubernetes Engine API
